        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01E3dCwYnEpwEL7hfxpv6Um8",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/flag-debt.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Flag Debt\\ndescription: Unreleased feature flags that persist after a rollout, multiplying test paths and operational burden.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Flag-Debt\\n  - concept/Testing\\n  - concept/Technical-Debt\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single source describing the problem; mechanism and remediation strategies not yet detailed.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Definition\\n\\nFlag debt is the accumulation of **unreleased feature flags** \\u2014 flags left in code after a rollout has completed and the feature is fully enabled. Each unconsolidated flag remains a branch in the code path tree, even if its condition is now deterministic.\\n\\n## The Problem\\n\\nOnce a feature flag completes its rollout (e.g. code is enabled for all users or environments), the flag should be removed: the code inside it merged into the main path, and the flag machinery deleted. When this cleanup does not happen:\\n\\n- Each flag doubles (or more) the number of distinct execution paths the test suite must cover.\\n- Over time, with dozens of unremoved flags, the test matrix becomes combinatorially explosive and increasingly impractical to exhaustively verify.\\n- Code review and reasoning about behavior becomes harder \\u2014 the \\u0022live\\u0022 code path is obscured by dead or near-dead conditionals.\\n- The operational complexity of the flag system itself accumulates: more flags to manage, more state to reason about, more potential for inconsistency.\\n\\nThis is flag debt: a technical liability that grows silently and compounds with each new flag left in place.\\n\\n## Cleanup Discipline\\n\\nThe antidote is **systematic flag removal** as part of the release ceremony:\\n\\n- Define a flag lifecycle: creation, rollout, **consolidation**, deletion.\\n- Assign ownership: the team that created the flag is responsible for its removal.\\n- Automate where possible: tooling that detects flags whose condition is now static (all true or all false) can prompt or auto-clean.\\n- Make removal a deployment checklist: never mark a feature as \\u0022live\\u0022 without a linked task to remove the flag.\\n- Audit periodically: scan for orphaned flags \\u2014 those that are always-on or always-off but still in code.\\n\\n## Relation to Deployment / Release Decoupling\\n\\nFeature flags enable deploying code without releasing it. Flag debt is the price of that flexibility when flag cleanup is deferred or forgotten. The decoupling itself is not the problem; the operational discipline to consolidate and remove flags is.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Source reference eval://steering-adoption/sample-09\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
